.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-account-update - Change the account's name and/or email


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 account update [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Change the account's display name and/or contact email. At least one of
--name / --email is required.

.PP
Both are unique across accounts: a value another account already holds is refused
with 409. A deactivated account, or a session signed in with a deactivated wallet,
cannot change them (403) — a revoked key must not be able to redirect the contact
email, which is where the merchant's operational notifications go.

.PP
No confirmation is asked: the change is not destructive and 'account update'
reverses it.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--email\fP=""
	New contact email (unique across accounts)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for update

.PP
\fB--name\fP=""
	New display name (unique across accounts)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--account\fP=""
	Account id (default: the logged-in session account; another account's needs an admin session)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 account update --name "Acme Store"
  rail0 account update --name "Acme Store" --email ops@acme.example
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-account(1)\fP
